WEBVTT

00:00:00.201 --> 00:00:06.001
One street photo can hold a pedestrian
twenty-four pixels tall and a bus four
hundred pixels wide.

00:00:06.001 --> 00:00:10.473
At stride thirty-two, every feature cell
covers a thirty-two pixel block,

00:00:10.474 --> 00:00:13.414
so the pedestrian is three quarters of one
cell.

00:00:13.413 --> 00:00:15.842
A stride-four map places it precisely,

00:00:15.843 --> 00:00:20.827
but there a three by three layer adds
eight pixels of context, not sixty-four.

00:00:20.826 --> 00:00:23.301
One map knows where, the other knows what.

00:00:23.301 --> 00:00:24.401
We need both.

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:26.587
The classical answer is a pyramid.

00:00:26.587 --> 00:00:30.900
Blur the image, keep every second pixel in
each direction, and repeat.

00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:32.212
The blur matters.

00:00:32.212 --> 00:00:36.464
Drop every second pixel of these fine
stripes, and they turn into false,

00:00:36.465 --> 00:00:37.351
coarse stripes.

00:00:37.350 --> 00:00:40.212
Blur first, and the false pattern is gone.

00:00:40.212 --> 00:00:42.844
Each level has a quarter of the pixels
below it,

00:00:42.845 --> 00:00:46.026
so the whole pyramid costs under four
thirds of the image.

00:00:46.005 --> 00:00:49.292
A Laplacian pyramid stores what each
halving lost.

00:00:49.292 --> 00:00:51.392
Take four, six, ten, two.

00:00:51.392 --> 00:00:52.442
Average each pair:

00:00:52.442 --> 00:00:53.692
five and six.

00:00:53.692 --> 00:00:54.442
Average again:

00:00:54.442 --> 00:00:55.842
five point five.

00:00:55.842 --> 00:00:58.192
Now repeat five and six to upsample:

00:00:58.192 --> 00:01:00.342
five, five, six, six.

00:01:00.342 --> 00:01:02.130
Subtract from the original.

00:01:02.130 --> 00:01:04.542
Minus one, one, four, minus four.

00:01:04.542 --> 00:01:08.130
That is L zero, the part the coarser level
cannot predict.

00:01:08.097 --> 00:01:09.147
A quick test.

00:01:09.147 --> 00:01:12.372
Set L zero to all zeros, then rebuild from
the top.

00:01:12.372 --> 00:01:13.960
Which four numbers come back?

00:01:13.960 --> 00:01:15.672
Pause here and work it out.

00:01:17.148 --> 00:01:21.973
You get five, five, six, six, and the
error is exactly L zero.

00:01:21.973 --> 00:01:25.210
On a real image, the finest level looks
like the edges.

00:01:25.210 --> 00:01:27.660
Without it, the picture goes soft.

00:01:27.660 --> 00:01:31.061
Keep every level and rebuilding is exact,
for any blur,

00:01:31.062 --> 00:01:34.524
because each step adds back exactly what
was subtracted.

00:01:34.498 --> 00:01:37.623
A ResNet already computes a pyramid in one
pass.

00:01:37.623 --> 00:01:40.661
But its fine maps are sharp with weak
features.

00:01:40.661 --> 00:01:43.611
A feature pyramid network adds a top-down
path.

00:01:43.611 --> 00:01:45.261
Start from P five.

00:01:45.261 --> 00:01:51.061
Each level upsamples the coarser map and
adds a one by one convolution of the
matching C map.

00:01:51.061 --> 00:01:56.319
Every level now has its own resolution and
the deep meaning, with 256 channels,

00:01:56.320 --> 00:01:58.849
so one detection head serves them all.

00:01:58.738 --> 00:02:01.650
Each box goes to the level that fits its
size.

00:02:01.650 --> 00:02:06.955
Take the square root of its area over 224,
take log base two, add four,

00:02:06.956 --> 00:02:08.076
and round down.

00:02:08.075 --> 00:02:10.950
A 224 box goes to P four.

00:02:10.950 --> 00:02:14.325
Half that goes to P three, and double it
to P five.

00:02:14.325 --> 00:02:18.615
A forty-pixel box gives about one point
five, rounds down to one,

00:02:18.616 --> 00:02:21.389
and is clamped to P two, the lowest level.

00:02:21.318 --> 00:02:24.105
Segmentation needs a label for every
pixel.

00:02:24.105 --> 00:02:26.105
U-Net's encoder goes down,

00:02:26.106 --> 00:02:32.569
from 256 pixels and 64 channels to a
bottleneck of sixteen pixels and 1024
channels.

00:02:32.568 --> 00:02:34.174
The decoder climbs back up,

00:02:34.175 --> 00:02:37.744
and at each scale it joins the encoder map
of the same size.

00:02:37.743 --> 00:02:44.755
At thirty-two pixels, 512 upsampled
channels plus 512 skipped channels make
1024.

00:02:44.755 --> 00:02:46.918
The skips bring back the location.

00:02:46.918 --> 00:02:49.018
The decoder brings the context.

00:02:48.971 --> 00:02:52.658
Both designs merge fine location with deep
context.

00:02:52.658 --> 00:02:57.283
FPN adds the two streams, so the channel
count stays the same.

00:02:57.283 --> 00:03:01.833
U-Net concatenates them and lets the next
convolution learn how to mix them.

00:03:01.833 --> 00:03:05.671
Either way, the network reads every scale
in a single pass.
